<p>On a whim, 24-year-old college dropout {% person "peter-wolf-toth" %}Peter Wolf Toth{% endperson %}—whose surname rhymes with "oath"—stopped along the California coast and began carving a giant head into a seaside cliff. When he returned the next day, a crowd had gathered. A local newspaper characterized the unfinished sculpture as an Indian head. In a 1989 interview, Toth acknowledged that he may have carved one subconsciously because he had always been interested in Native people.</p>

<p>After returning to his adopted hometown of Akron, Ohio, Toth carved a second head in the stump of a dead elm tree in a city park. He eventually conceived a mission to create a Native American sculpture in each state, calling the project the Trail of the Whispering Giants. He intended the works to draw attention to Native American history and to experiences of displacement that he felt paralleled his own childhood.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/whispering-giant-statue/304-img-2956.jpg" alt="Detailed view of Mandan's Whispering Giant sculpture">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Except for his first sculpture in California, Toth carved the monuments from local wood. He described the Mandan figure as a composite representing North Dakota's Native nations. After abandoning an initial attempt using a hollow, deteriorated tree in Jaycee Park, the Helbling family donated a healthy cottonwood nearby. The Mandan people had used cottonwood in building earthlodges. The completed sculpture was later treated with preservatives to protect it from the weather.</p>

<p>The sculpture was formally dedicated and presented to the people of North Dakota on November 24, 1981. Toth originally intended to place it at the Heritage Center in Bismarck, but delays associated with selecting a state-owned site led him to accept a location in southern Mandan. The Helbling family donated a corner of a business parking lot with the intention of transferring the land to the local park district.</p>

<p>Toth favored the publicly accessible location along the route to the Standing Rock Indian Reservation, near the Highway 1806 bridge across the Heart River and the Mandan village at Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park.</p>

